{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785183928,"nonce":"bC3b/ewgpCN0+a+qZxmpU4FyZ4E=","message":"**Answered by ADR-0062 part 3 — and this ticket's premise was wrong.** Closing in favour of cb53ca9.\n\nThe premise was that hashing only the writable upper layer 'needs the same privileged mount' as lazy reads, i.e. a mount on the **node**. It does not: put the `overlayfs` on the **workspace service** instead, over a Snapshot Farm on its own disk, and the upper directory holds precisely the paths the Step touched — put there by the kernel — with the drain reading it locally and no network in the path.\n\nSo the drain becomes **exact rather than approximate**, which also retires the hazard in the unprivileged approximation that was going to be built instead: a `(size, mtime)` stat cache whose failure mode is silently publishing a stale hash on an mtime race. That stat cache is still being built, but correctly scoped as the fallback for configurations with no Export (the local executor) rather than as the mechanism.\n\nVerified on colima/ext4 that copy-up leaves the lower layer's CAS blob unchanged, so a Step cannot corrupt the store by writing through its Export.","files":null}]}